00:01:11.516 --> 00:01:16.495
(metal music)

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.080
Welcome to another episode of Things&nbsp;
You Missed. My name is Zac, aka CZ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.680
and I am analyzing the entire Halloween&nbsp;
franchise here on this channel. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.840
are many different routes you can travel on the&nbsp;
Halloween Express. Currently, after Halloween 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.840
you can choose to continue on to Halloween&nbsp;
4-6, or you can opt for a different reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.400
where nothing happens for the next 20&nbsp;
years until Halloween H20. In this episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.560
no surprises, I’ll once again be aided&nbsp;
by Halloween expert Jimmy Champane.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.440
– [Jimmy] Directed by Steve Miner, this film&nbsp;
represents a semi-rebirth of the franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.120
It was the first Halloween movie to be released&nbsp;
in Summer, it takes place in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.840
fictional town called Summer Glen, the first&nbsp;
return to the Golden State since Halloween III.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.880
The title, H-twenty resembles H2O, the chemical&nbsp;
compound name for water. Despite that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.680
there’s no pools, no beaches and we don’t&nbsp;even
get to see Michael Myers cutting up any girls in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.080
bikinis. Instead, it takes place in a boarding&nbsp;
school where Laurie Strode lives a secret life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.040
as a headmistress under a false name. This is&nbsp;
now the third alias she’s used: Laurie Strode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.480
Laurie Myers, and now Keri Tate, all to evade her&nbsp;
murderous brother, whose name precedes him. So go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.640
ahead and grab some sunscreen and a towel,&nbsp;
and let’s dive into the Things You Missed.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.265
(knives slash)
(50s music)

00:02:30.374 --> 00:02:35.280
– [Zac]&nbsp;The opening song is Mr. Sandman by the Chordettes,&nbsp;
the same song used in Halloween 2, where it tied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.960
into themes about dreams and the unconscious&nbsp;
mind. In this case, it’s more of a callback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.600
and maybe a slight reminder that Halloween 2&nbsp;
is still canon, while 4-6 are not. We actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.360
hear the song again later, in Laurie’s car,&nbsp;
and we see her react to it and turn it off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.600
It’s almost like she’s reacting to her painful&nbsp;
memories of Halloween 2, which wouldn’t make any sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.520
because the song was never used diegetically,&nbsp;
meaning that we the audience could hear it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.440
but the characters did not. So, I don’t know,&nbsp;
maybe Laurie is just not a Chordettes fan.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.320
The part where Laurie gets startled by the&nbsp;
little girl in the clown costume is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.506
more understandable, because it’s well known that&nbsp;
Michael wore a clown costume for his first attack.

00:03:06.506 --> 00:03:09.440
Back to the opening, we see that&nbsp;
Nurse Marion is still a smoker, and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.440
too has taken on a new last name: Whittington.&nbsp;
It’s not clear if this is also for protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.280
or if she just married at some point.&nbsp;
After realizing her home has been INVADED,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.400
she goes to the neighbors for help, and&nbsp;
they have a Halloween decoration of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.280
of the cenobites from the Hellraiser&nbsp;
franchise. It’s Butterball, the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.640
that looks like the gritty live action&nbsp;
version of Patrick Star from SpongeBob,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.160
if he were in a Matrix movie. Hellraiser,&nbsp;
from the third movie onward, was produced or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.560
distributed by Dimension Films, who also did the&nbsp;
90s and 2000s Halloween films -- you may remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.240
the Pinhead pumpkin I pointed out in the last&nbsp;
movie, Halloween: The Curse of Michael Myers.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.880
– [Jimmy] Dude, if they ever remake this movie, instead&nbsp;
of Butterball they should definitely use Harvey Weinstein.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.400
– [Zac] There’s another horror icon referenced&nbsp;
moments later when we see this kid dressed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.880
as Jason from Friday the 13th. H20 director&nbsp;
Steve Miner also did the second and third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.960
Friday the 13th films. Also, the Detective&nbsp;
in H20 is named Fitzsimmons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.080
the same name as the police chief&nbsp;
from Friday the 13th Part III.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.600
The address of Marion’s house is also a&nbsp;
reference to one of Steve Miner’s films.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.600
– Yeah, yeah. It’s 4946 Cypress Pond.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.720
– [Zac] The 4946 comes from the real location where&nbsp;
this scene was shot, 4946 Melrose Hill Street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.000
and Cypress Pond is an address&nbsp;
referenced in Steve Miner’s 1992&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.720
film Forever Young, which&nbsp;
also stars Jamie Lee Curtis

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.840
– Susan Finley's address is 14 Cypress Pond Road.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.520
– Miner also has an uncredited cameo as&nbsp;
this very apologetic school official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.640
who tells Molly about her financial aid situation.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.200
– [Miner] He said he wasn’t going to be there.&nbsp;
I’m very sorry, I wish I could help.&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.880
– [Molly] But he promised that--
– [Miner] No.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.760
– Brutal. One of the teens that helps&nbsp;
Marion is wearing a hockey jersey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:37.680
with an L and the number 17 on it. The&nbsp;
name Laurie starts with an L and she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.120
17 during the original Halloween. Not sure&nbsp;
if that is what they were going for there.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.520
– [Jimmy] Either way, he’s about to take a big L&nbsp;
from our boy Michael Myers. His name is Allegre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.000
a French word meaning happy, joyful or cheerful.&nbsp;
All of which he is... until he dies.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.720
It turns out Michael ransacked Marion’s office,&nbsp;
there are papers everywhere and the file on Laurie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.400
Strode has been emptied. This scene is the&nbsp;
parallel to Halloween 4, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.120
Michael goes through Jamie’s photo box and finds
out that she’s related to Laurie Strode. Marion tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.640
to turn on the lights, but realizes that the power has been
cut; which is another thing that Michael does in Halloween 4.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.760
Marion also has a picture of Loomis. When&nbsp;
we see him, the music resembles music from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.120
the original film. The picture is also&nbsp;
an homage to Donald Pleasence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.920
who unfortunately died shortly after making&nbsp;Halloween 6:
The Curse of Michael Myers.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.160
One by one, Michael takes out everyone&nbsp;
involved, starting with the teenage neighbors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.760
One of them gets a skate through the head&nbsp;
while watching Plan 9 From Outer Space, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.800
seems to be an intentional callback to The original Halloween when Laurie and Tommy are watching The Thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.400
From Another World.
Both of these movies are sci-fi films from the 50s.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.920
– [Zac] There’s also this license plate:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:46.000
ITA1959. The same year Plan 9 From Outer Space&nbsp;
came out. Coincidence? I think, probably.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.920
And once again, Michael’s attack on Nurse Marion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.880
results in him stealing a car.&nbsp;
Grand Theft Auto: Haddonfield.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.160
During the opening credits, we see Marion’s&nbsp;
collection of Michael Myers memorabilia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.840
which she inherited from Dr. Loomis. We see&nbsp;
articles from after his initial attack on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.640
older sister in ‘63, and this one, dated the&nbsp;
day after the main events of Halloween (1978),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.440
before it goes into speculations&nbsp;
about if Michael is still alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.800
There are photos of people we’ve never seen&nbsp;
before, which seems to suggest that Loomis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.400
or Marion suspected Michael of having&nbsp;
other victims during the 20 year gap.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.280
There are a couple mistakes in these&nbsp;
articles, like the one that says “Laurie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.120
Strode sole survivor of October Mass&nbsp;
Murders”. This forgets about Loomis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.680
Tommy, Lindsey and Jimmy, all of whom survive.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:27.440
– [Jimmy] Not me Jimmy. The movie Jimmy. But not the&nbsp;
H2O Jimmy. The old Jimmy. Too many Jimmys.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.600
– [Zac] This article incorrectly states&nbsp;
that the Halloween murders occured in 1968,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.680
it was actually ‘78. Then there’s this&nbsp;
one. “Authorities have no explanation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.000
the disappearance of the headstone of Judith&nbsp;
Myers from Memorial Cemetery.” No explanation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.040
huh? You don’t think maybe it was… I don’t know,&nbsp;
Judith’s brother? The same guy responsible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.720
killing all those teenagers, one of whom&nbsp;
was arranged under that very tombstone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.680
at the crime scene that same day? Not the most&nbsp;
excellent deductive skills from Haddonfield PD.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:57.440
I went through the movie shot by shot to see&nbsp;
what additional lore these articles contain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:00.800
What I found was the truth behind how&nbsp;
Michael escaped all of those years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:03.040
but also, a lot of other weird stuff.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:07.399
(impact)

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:15.520
If you watch Things You Missed regularly,&nbsp;
there are a lot of details in newspapers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.640
that come up on this show. Oftentimes,&nbsp;
we see a headline, but the content of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:21.920
the article is totally unrelated, because&nbsp;
when you’re watching the movie in real time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.680
you don’t have time to read the whole&nbsp;
article. This one, from November 1963,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.520
starts as a legit story about Michael&nbsp;
Myers, then changes topics mid-sentence.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.320
“He is currently undergoing&nbsp;
tests for a number of disorders. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.680
boy has no history of violence. With&nbsp;
the advent of modern medical imaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.880
third term fetuses have been observed&nbsp;
doing complicated dance movements.”

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.760
I sometimes find it frustrating&nbsp;
when the filmmakers can’t be bothered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.680
just write a fake in-universe article, but&nbsp;
in this case, he was so close to filling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.560
required space that the article takes&nbsp;
up, that it’s making me think the prop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.040
designers actually do this weird stuff on&nbsp;
purpose, as an inside joke or trivia item.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.880
This other article tells the story of how Michael&nbsp;
actually escaped the sanitarium all those years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.960
ago, by climbing out a second story window and&nbsp;
descending on a handmade rope consisting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.960
stolen bedsheets, like something out of a cartoon.&nbsp;
I don’t really know what the point of all that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.880
considering that Michael is invincible and can&nbsp;
pretty quickly recover from any fall damage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.600
as we see again towards the end&nbsp;
of H20, when Laurie knocks him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.840
off of a balcony as a callback to the&nbsp;
original, and he survives both times.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:18.720
– [Jimmy] They didn’t even have to come up with&nbsp;
something new to explain how he escaped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.080
It’s spelled out in the first movie&nbsp;
when Marion and Loomis show up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.120
Michael lets the inmates out and escapes in the&nbsp;
chaos. We literally see them walking around!

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.600
– [Zac] Now that you mention it, yeah, you’re&nbsp;
100% right. Once again, this article is 90%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.840
legit, until they randomly start talking about&nbsp;
Michael doing these “complicated dance movements”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:39.280
I guess they call him the boogeyman because he&nbsp;
knows how to boogie. At least they know how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.280
spell his name correctly though, unlike this&nbsp;
article which spells it like the soap brand.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.720
Just when I thought I’d finally found a&nbsp;
decent article about death row inmates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:50.240
the story randomly starts talking&nbsp;
about “third term fetuses doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.360
complicated dance movements”. It’s&nbsp;
gotta be some kind of inside joke.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.240
– [Jimmy] One interesting detail in terms of lore&nbsp;
is the article that states Laurie died in an auto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.080
accident. Not only is this how Laurie’s parents&nbsp;
died, but it’s also how she died over in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.880
Halloween 4-6 timeline. This leads me to believe&nbsp;
that this is a holdover from an earlier revision 

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:11.760
of Halloween H20 script. The movie was originally&nbsp;
planned to be a sequel to Halloween 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.760
before the idea of a new timeline was decided on. If you're looking really close

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:17.920
you can see the stained&nbsp;
scissors Halloween 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:24.214
but, obviously, that movie didn't happen in this timeline, so we're just going to go ahead and assume that they were a pair&nbsp;of scissors used 

00:09:24.214 --> 00:09:27.235
in the 20 year gap&nbsp;
where Loomis was hunting down Michael.

00:09:27.235 --> 00:09:30.210
Or it could be an intentional easter egg to Halloween 4.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:31.787
I just don't want to give them that much credit.

00:09:31.787 --> 00:09:34.880
– [Zac] After the opening credits, we see&nbsp;
a long POV shot, a callback to the way the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:38.720
original Halloween began. You can also see white&nbsp;
sheets blowing in the breeze. White sheets were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:42.400
a psychosexual motif that I talked about in the&nbsp;
original Halloween, I’ve pointed out how they come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.320
back in nearly every Halloween movie since. This&nbsp;
all turns out to be part of Laurie’s nightmare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.080
and it does a nice job of setting up the horror&nbsp;
well before she sees this hauntingly familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.960
image when Michael is actually coming&nbsp;
after them towards the end of the movie.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:56.400
Laurie is now an English teacher, which is&nbsp;
fitting after we saw her excel in her high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.600
school English class back in the original.&nbsp;
There’s a reimagining of that scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.440
where her son’s girlfriend Molly is sitting in the&nbsp;
back corner and she sees Michael out the window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.960
the same thing that happened to Laurie when&nbsp;
she sat in that spot two decades prior. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:10.560
Laurie calls on Molly to answer her question&nbsp;
about Frankenstein, Molly talks about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:14.400
Victor Frankenstein should have confronted his&nbsp;
monster sooner to prevent Elizabeth’s death.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.200
– I mean, the monster saw to that&nbsp;
by killing off everybody that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:21.280
loved. Victor finally had to face it. It&nbsp;
was about redemption. It was his fate.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.920
– [Jimmy] If you recall, during that Halloween&nbsp;
afternoon in 1978, Laurie’s curriculum also talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:31.840
about the idea of “fate” in the class that she's in. In the&nbsp;
original, fate is described as an immovable force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:36.720
In other words, Michael was an immovable force of nature&nbsp;
that would come for Laurie, and there was nothing&nbsp;at all

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:42.320
she could do to stop it. All she could do was run&nbsp;
and hide. But in H20, fate is still portrayed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:47.120
an inevitable confrontation, but Molly suggests&nbsp;
that the character, Victor Frankenstein, has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:52.480
face his fate in order to redeem himself, which is&nbsp;
exactly what we see Laurie do at the most critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.720
point in the story. This version of Laurie has&nbsp;
been running and hiding every step of the way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:01.760
Even when she has to confront Michael in the first movie,&nbsp;
it's usually done in self-defense.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:04.800
– [Zac] There’s even a banner in her&nbsp;
classroom that says Learn from the Past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.720
Live in the Present, Plan for the Future, but&nbsp;
she doesn’t follow that advice herself. It’s very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:12.480
clear that she’s stuck in the past. She spends&nbsp;
her whole life in fear of her brother’s return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:16.000
and there comes a point where she has the kids&nbsp;
in the car with an opportunity to run away once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:19.600
again. But she remembers her own teachings&nbsp;
in the English class, and instead of fleeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:24.000
she goes back to kill her monster. This character&nbsp;
arc is three movies in the making for her.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.760
But before we get there, let’s talk about&nbsp;
life at Hillcrest Academy. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:31.840
with that fancy font, it almost looks more&nbsp;
like Killcrest Academy. Where do I enroll?

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:34.320
– So are you studying to&nbsp;
be a killer? Or a final boy?

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:37.520
– No comment. Laurie is very&nbsp;
overprotective of her teenage son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:41.040
and as a side effect, kind of prevents&nbsp;
him from living his life. As this unfolds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.800
we see her grab the box of Life cereal,&nbsp;
and put it away for safekeeping. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:48.480
feels somehow symbolic. Even John’s&nbsp;
friends make fun of him for it.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:50.720
– Twenty years from now you’re&nbsp;
still gonna be living with her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:53.600
Probably running some weird motel&nbsp;
out in the middle of nowhere.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:56.640
– Yep, it wouldn’t be a Things You Missed&nbsp;
episode if there wasn’t some kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:01.360
reference to Hitchcock’s 1960 horror classic,&nbsp;
Psycho. If you somehow haven’t seen it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.640
it’s about a character named Norman Bates,&nbsp;
who at one point lives with his mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:08.800
Norma Bates and they run the appropriately&nbsp;
titled family business: The Bates Motel.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.680
Jamie Lee Curtis, who plays Laurie,&nbsp;
is the daughter of Janet Leigh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:15.840
who stars in Psycho, and she also makes&nbsp;
a cameo in H20, where her name is Norma.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:17.760
– [Norma] Oh good morning Ms. Tate.
– Good morning, Norma.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.760
Norma is the one to deliver this line…

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:26.320
– Well, it's Halloween. I guess&nbsp;
everyone is entitled to one good scare.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:29.040
– [Zac] …which is the same iconic line that&nbsp;
Sheriff Brackett has in the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:31.920
and later on in Halloween&nbsp;
Kills. During that conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.120
Norma senses Laurie’s distress&nbsp;
and offers some maternal advice.

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:40.720
– I know it’s not my place … If&nbsp;
I could be maternal for a moment…

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.920
…Which is funny, because, again, that&nbsp;
is her actual mother in real life.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:48.640
– It’s all so corny. It kills me every time I see it.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.200
– As she walks away, we hear&nbsp;
a little piece of Bernard Hermann’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.720
musical score from Psycho and she returns&nbsp;
to her vehicle, which is just like the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:58.080
her character drives in Psycho. It even&nbsp;
has the same license plate number. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:01.920
was probably enough of a Psycho homage,&nbsp;
but hey, why not add one more in there?

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:03.440
– Psycho.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.480
– I also noticed some music by&nbsp;
H20’s composer John Ottman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.920
that isn’t from Psycho, but is very&nbsp;
reminiscent of Bernard Hermann’s style.

00:13:10.088 --> 00:13:15.100
(eerie music)

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:20.722
– Unfortunately for Ottman, some of his music was taken out and literally just straight up replaced with &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.722 --> 00:13:24.320
music from Scream. The scene where&nbsp;
Michael chases them through the corridors uses the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:29.520
music from the garage scene in Scream, and part&nbsp;
of the intro with Nurse Marion uses music from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:35.200
Casey Becker intro in Scream. In fact, you probably
realized that H20’s intro itself is very

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:40.720
similar to the intro from Scream. Both involve a woman home&nbsp;
alone and realizes she’s being stalked at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:46.000
front and back door, who then contacts the police, but&nbsp;
before they arrive, runs outside to try to escape.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:50.960
– In fact, there are many elements of H20&nbsp;
that just scream Scream, from the contemporary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:55.200
media references to the Northern California&nbsp;
setting. And there are two reasons for this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.200 --> 00:14:00.000
One. Scream came out in 1996 and revitalized&nbsp;
the slasher genre in a way that 1995’s Curse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.280
of Michael Myers did not. Kevin&nbsp;
Williamson was heavily involved in writing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:07.520
script, despite not getting the final on screen&nbsp;
credit. Two. The original Halloween was heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:11.600
featured in Scream as the movie that the kids&nbsp;
all watch at Stu Macher’s party. The Halloween&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:15.760
franchise would link back to Scream by showing&nbsp;
Scream 2 as the movie that Molly and Sarah have on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:20.560
TV in their dorm room. Scream 2 is also the Scream&nbsp;
movie that takes place on campus, much like H20.

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.880
If you’ve seen my Scream Things You&nbsp;
Missed, you know all about how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.680
intro of Scream has a part where Mr.&nbsp;
Becker discovers their home has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:29.240
broken into and tells his wife to go down to&nbsp;
the Mackenzie’s house and call the police.

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:34.160
– Get in the car. Drive down to&nbsp;
the MacKenzies’. Call the police.

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.840
– This was a reference to the original Halloween,&nbsp;
where Laurie tells Tommy and Lindsey to go call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:41.440
the police from the Mackenzies’, so in&nbsp;
H20, the Halloween franchise responds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:44.000
by having Laurie tell the kids to&nbsp;
call the police from the Beckers’.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.520
– I want you to drive down the street&nbsp;
to the Beckers’, a mile down the road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:49.760
Tell them to call an ambulance and get the police.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:53.280
– [Zac] There was even a connection between&nbsp;
Ghostface and Michael Myers. H20 Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.880
was played by Chris Durand, who was under&nbsp;
the Ghostface mask for parts of Scream 2.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:00.560
As much as I could go on about the newfound&nbsp;
relationship between Halloween and Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:04.320
we haven’t even begun to talk about who Laurie&nbsp;
was talking to on the phone, connections to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:08.183
original movie that you might have overlooked,&nbsp;
and the rest of the Things You Missed.

00:15:08.183 --> 00:15:12.097
(impact)

00:15:13.199 --> 00:15:16.037
(mysterious music)

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:20.320
The group of students staying back from&nbsp;
Hillcrest Academy’s Yosemite field trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:23.840
consists of two couples. In the original&nbsp;
Halloween, there were also two teen couples:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:27.760
Annie and Paul being the first; Lynda and Bob&nbsp;
being the second. Like the teens from Halloween,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.240
these kids only have one thing on their minds.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.140
– Food. *wink*

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:33.840
– [Charlie] Whole place will be empty. We could have the run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:37.440
of it. We could have a roaming orgy!
– [Sarah] I like the way this man thinks!

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:40.560
– Unlike last time however,&nbsp;
this time Laurie is also getting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:43.280
on the romantic activities. She’s&nbsp;
dating the guidance counselor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:46.320
Mr. Brennan, who brings a pumpkin over&nbsp;
to her dwelling on Halloween night.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:47.840
– I thought we’d do this right.

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:51.920
History is kind of repeating itself here. In&nbsp;
1978, Laurie brought a pumpkin along to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:55.520
babysitting job to carve with Tommy. There are&nbsp;
a number of small moments like this that serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:59.600
as homages to Halloween. For example, after&nbsp;
hanging Sarah, Michael does the same head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:03.840
tilt thing that he did after killing Bob Simms in&nbsp;
the original. Almost as if he’s admiring his work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:07.440
Actually, the very idea of hanging Sarah&nbsp;
from above, like a Halloween decoration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:11.040
calls back to what Michael later did with&nbsp;
Bob, where he was strung up in a closet.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.920
The moment where Laurie and Michael come&nbsp;
face-to-face in the window harkens back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:17.920
to the brief eye contact they made twenty years&nbsp;
prior when Laurie pulled off her brother’s mask.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:21.040
Not long after, she hides the kids in a&nbsp;
closet, the same strat she used to hide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.320
Tommy and Lindsey all those years&nbsp;
ago. She even uses the same line.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:26.160
– Do as I say, now!

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.600
– Mr. Brennan’s death involves him being stabbed&nbsp;
and lifted off the ground with the knife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:34.080
the same fate that we saw befall the nurse from&nbsp;
Halloween 2. And temporarily knocking out Michael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.680
Laurie makes the same mistake she made in the&nbsp;
original by dropping the knife. At the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:41.520
we see Michael get brake-yeeted out the&nbsp;
front windshield, as he was in Halloween 4.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:44.480
Before the rampage, Mr. Brennan goes&nbsp;
to check on all the students remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:47.440
on the campus. But did anyone else&nbsp;
find his word choice kind of ominous?

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:52.960
– [Mr. Brennan] Sarah. Molly. Just&nbsp;
checking on the Hillcrest remains.

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:56.800
– A little strange, considering one of them&nbsp;
would soon become a pile of human remains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:00.080
And then when John makes the little map for&nbsp;
Molly and sends it down the dumbwaiter… it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:03.760
looks innocent enough… but what’s this?&nbsp;
I recognize this from our Purge videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:06.800
that’s an Iron Cross. John Tate is Nazi confirmed.

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:09.600
Also, I think I found where they&nbsp;
got the title of this movie from.

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:15.840
– Twenty years. Twenty. Years. Don’t you&nbsp;
think he would have shown up by now?

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:17.040
– [Zac] No, obviously not.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:20.000
After classes get out that afternoon,&nbsp;
we see Laurie making a phone call as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:23.520
the buses are loaded for Yosemite. And&nbsp;
I just realized it’s a BellSouth phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:27.360
same as what Casey Becker uses in Scream.&nbsp;
She’s talking to a woman named Mrs. Cheney.

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:29.120
– Mrs. Chaney, can I call you right back?

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:32.560
– The name Chaney is usually eponymous in&nbsp;
the horror genre, because of Lon Chaney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:36.160
probably the most iconic silent horror movie&nbsp;
actor of all time. Chaney was known for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:39.520
portrayal of an array of monsters, which&nbsp;
is most fitting for this Halloween movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:43.040
where the central theme is so focused on&nbsp;
Laurie going back to kill her monster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:46.160
Unfortunately, Mr. Brennan nearly&nbsp;
shoots an innocent man in the process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:49.360
He got a little too jumpy when the security&nbsp;
guard Ronnie came up to check on them.

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:53.040
Actually, if we go back and slow down that scene,&nbsp;
it actually is Michael approaching in that first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:57.360
shot, but when we cut back after Brennan fires&nbsp;
the gun, it’s now Ronnie. At first it seems kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:01.040
of like the movie is trying to cheat the audience,&nbsp;
but when you think back, it actually is consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:05.040
with how hallucinations are handled earlier in the&nbsp;
film. There are instances where Laurie has visions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:09.440
of Michael, and has to close her eyes for a moment&nbsp;
to realize it’s actually someone else. Steve Miner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:13.280
made the choice to show us what the characters&nbsp;
think they see, rather than showing the objective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:16.880
truth, and I think this is a good tactic to make&nbsp;
the audience feel like they’re more in the moment.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.760
This is also not the first time or&nbsp;
last time someone has been mistaken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:22.640
for The Boogeyman. Ben Tramer got&nbsp;
killed for wearing the mask in 2,

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:24.320
– RIP Ben Tramer

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:25.835
– [Zac] Ted Hollister got shot in 4…

00:18:25.835 --> 00:18:27.840
(sad music),

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:32.080
Spitz was killed by Michael Myers&nbsp;
after dressing up as him in five…

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:34.960
(more sad music)

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:37.520
Freddy Harris nearly got beat&nbsp;
down in Resurrection, … we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:40.960
don’t really care about that one… and&nbsp;
Mr. Tivoli got angry-mobbed in Kills.

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:42.560
– That one was just weird.

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:45.840
– As much as I love H20’s story, and how&nbsp;
it does something different with the Halloween&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:49.520
formula, it’s hard to get past the fact that&nbsp;
Michael Myers just doesn’t look very scary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:52.960
in it. A lot of this goes back to the mask.&nbsp;
I could try to talk about this, but I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:56.880
someone who studies the Michael Myers masks like&nbsp;
his life depends on it. Here’s Jimmy Champane.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:01.120
– This movie has a very similar problem&nbsp;
to Halloween 4 where I like the vast majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:06.770
the characters, the kills, the setting, and pretty much everything about it is cool, but there's one glaring issue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:13.200
holding it back and that is the mask. Or in this&nbsp;
case, masks. By our count Halloween H20 uses 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:19.040
real masks and one CGI monstrosity. None of them&nbsp;
are what I would call good but some are better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:23.520
than others. Either way, Michael’s overall look&nbsp;
in this movie is a mess. To me at least.

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:26.000
– [Zac] Outside of the mask, there are&nbsp;
a couple of other moments that took away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.720
from the horror. I love when Laurie walks&nbsp;
through the corridor looking for Michael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:32.640
but then we turn back and see him just lowering&nbsp;
himself down with one arm. I like how they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:36.400
added a new move to his repertoire, but&nbsp;
like… where was he when Laurie walked by?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:39.040
There’s not that much space right here.&nbsp;
I need to see this whole maneuver.

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:42.000
And then when she’s hiding under the&nbsp;
tables. I don’t get why he can’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.960
bend down to see where she's hiding. I&nbsp;
know that wouldn’t be as suspenseful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:47.520
but it doesn’t make sense that&nbsp;
he doesn’t think to do this.

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:50.480
Ultimately, this allows Laurie to&nbsp;
stab him with the California flag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:54.400
which is probably symbolic of something but I&nbsp;
don’t really know, and later, drive him off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:59.840
cliff and chop his head off, finally putting an&nbsp;
end to her 20 year war with Michael Myers. Or so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:04.640
we thought. Because Michael Myers cannot&nbsp;
die as long as you have $20 in your pocket.

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:10.000
Next up on Things You Missed: a Resurrection&nbsp;
would soon be upon us. Someone kill me.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.400
– Even I cannot defend Resurrection.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:16.480
– [Zac] Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for&nbsp;
new horrors every week, ring the deathbell and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:23.267
select all notifications. I will… I suppose… see&nbsp;
you in the next one. Assuming we both survive.

00:20:23.267 --> 00:20:33.592
(spooky dance music)

